## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to reason about data-parallel bit tricks and boundary-safe shifts without branches.

## Key Concepts

- Byte-wise (SWAR) operations across 128-bit lanes
- Constructing per-byte masks to constrain operations to byte boundaries
- Handling n in 0..7 without loops or conditionals

## Code Example

```cpp
// outline of approach (conceptual; leverages 128-bit value split into bytes)
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint128 perByteRotateLeft128(uint128 x, unsigned n){
    uint128 M = { 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFULL, 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFULL };
    uint128 left  = { .hi = (x.hi << n) | (x.lo >> (8 - n)), .lo = x.lo << n };
    uint128 right = { .hi = (x.hi >> (8 - n)), .lo = (x.lo >> (8 - n)) };
    return { .hi = (left.hi & M.hi) | (right.hi & ~M.hi),
             .lo = (left.lo & M.lo) | (right.lo & ~M.lo) };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you generalize to 32-bit lanes or to 256-bit types?
- What are the performance implications on modern compilers and ISAs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests mastery of branchless, scalable bit-twiddling techniques for 64-bit inputs.

## Key Concepts
- SWAR style bit reversal
- Masking and shifting sequences
- Branchless algorithms for performance

## Code Example
```c
#include <stdint.h>

uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t n) {
  n = ((n >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL) | ((n & 0x5555555555555555ULL) << 1);
  n = ((n >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL) | ((n & 0x3333333333333333ULL) << 2);
  n = ((n >> 4) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) | ((n & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0FULL) << 4);
  n = ((n >> 8) & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) | ((n & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8);
  n = ((n >> 16) & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) | ((n & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16);
  n = (n >> 32) | (n << 32);
  return n;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you optimize for smaller word sizes or specialized ISAs?
- How would you adapt for 128-bit inputs and performance considerations?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests bitwise reasoning and ensures a constant-time, loop-free parity computation relevant to integrity checks.

## Key Concepts
- XOR-fold parity reductions without loops
- Endianness-neutral bit tricks; GCC/Clang optimizations

## Code Example
```javascript
unsigned parity64(uint64_t x) {
  x ^= x >> 32;
  x ^= x >> 16;
  x ^= x >> 8;
  x ^= x >> 4;
  x ^= x >> 2;
  x ^= x >> 1;
  return x & 1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend to 128-bit?
- How to verify correctness with random testing and edge cases?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of 128-bit data manipulation, endianness, and composing simple bit tricks to achieve a larger transformation without loops.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit value composed of two 64-bit halves
- Byte reversal within 64-bit words (endian swap)
- Combining results to form final 128-bit reversal
- No loops; relies on fixed-mask shifts

## Code Example

```javascript
uint64_t reverse64(uint64_t x){
  x = ((x & 0x00000000FFFFFFFFULL) << 32) | ((x & 0xFFFFFFFF00000000ULL) >> 32);
  x = ((x & 0x0000FFFF0000FFFFULL) << 16) | ((x & 0xFFFF0000FFFF0000ULL) >> 16);
  x = ((x & 0x00FF00FF00FF00FFULL) << 8)  | ((x & 0xFF00FF00FF00FF00ULL) >> 8);
  return x;
}

void endianSwap128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo){
  *out_hi = reverse64(lo);
  *out_lo = reverse64(hi);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you verify correctness for non-trivial patterns quickly?
- How does this approach scale if you extend to 256-bit values?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of 128-bit SWAR techniques and per-byte manipulation, not simply whole-value reversal.

## Key Concepts

- SWAR bit-reversal
- Mask design across 16 bytes
- Branch-free parallel bit-twiddling

## Code Example

```c
uint128 reverseBits128(uint128 x){
  const uint128 M1 = ...; // 0x5555... across 128 bits
  const uint128 M2 = ...; // 0x3333...
  const uint128 M4 = ...; // 0x0F0F...
  // stage 1: swap adj bits
  x = (x >> 1) & M1 | (x & M1) << 1;
  // stage 2: swap bit-pairs
  x = (x >> 2) & M2 | (x & M2) << 2;
  // stage 3: swap nibbles
  x = (x >> 4) & M4 | (x & M4) << 4;
  // stage 4: reverse within bytes
  // (use an appropriate mask like M8)
  return x;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt for endianness in memory when reading/writing?
- What are the performance implications on CPUs lacking 128-bit intrinsics?

## Why This Is Asked
This checks understanding of fixed-sequence bit tricks and performance constraints in bitfield work.

## Key Concepts
- Hamming weight via parallel summation
- Masks like 0x5555555555555555, 0x3333333333333333, 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0F

## Code Example
```javascript
// Implementation using BigInt for 64-bit precision
function popcount64(x) {
  x = (x) & 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFn;
  x = x - ((x >> 1n) & 0x5555555555555555n);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333n) + ((x >> 2n) & 0x3333333333333333n);
  x = (x + (x >> 4n)) & 0x0F0F0F0F0F0F0F0Fn;
  x = x + (x >> 8n);
  x = x + (x >> 16n);
  x = x + (x >> 32n);
  return Number(x & 0x7Fn);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- What are the trade-offs vs lookup-table popcount in fixed-width code?
- How would you adapt this for SIMD or wider widths?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to reason about cross-half bit patterns and leverage a reversal primitive to detect palindromicity without loops.

## Key Concepts

- 128-bit representations split across two 64-bit halves
- Cross-half bit equality, palindrome concept
- Using an existing reversal primitive to avoid per-bit work

## Code Example

```javascript
// Implementation sketch
int isPalindrome128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo) {
  uint64_t hi_rev, lo_rev;
  reverse128(hi, lo, &hi_rev, &lo_rev);
  return (hi == hi_rev && lo == lo_rev) ? 1 : 0;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- Could you implement a pure bitwise variant that does not rely on reverse128?
- How would you generalize to 256-bit values with four halves?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests low-level bitboard thinking, edge masking, and 128-bit manipulation without loops.

## Key Concepts
- 8x16 bitboard mapping in 128 bits (bit i = row*16 + col).
- Horizontal moves use 1-bit shifts with edge masks (COL0/COL15).
- Vertical moves use 16-bit shifts with TOP/BOTTOM masks.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode close to C for clarity
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
uint128 expandMask128(uint128 m){
  uint128 left  = (m & ~COL0) >> 1;
  uint128 right = (m & ~COL15) << 1;
  uint128 up    = (m & ~TOP) << 16;
  uint128 down  = (m & ~BOTTOM) >> 16;
  return or128(or128(left, right), or128(up, down));
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for an irregular 3x5 tile grid embedded in 128 bits?
- What are the performance implications of branching-free bitboard ops on modern CPUs?

## Why This Is Asked
Rotations are fundamental in cryptography (e.g., SHA-2), hashing, and bitboard manipulation. Implementing them correctly on composite 128-bit types tests understanding of cross-word boundaries and careful handling of edge cases.

## Key Concepts
- Decompose 128-bit value into hi/lo 64-bit halves
- Handle k near 64 where rotation crosses half boundaries
- No loops required—just careful bitwise arithmetic
- Return early for k==0 to avoid undefined behavior with 64-bit shifts

## Code Example
```c
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;

uint128 rotl128(uint128 x, unsigned k) {
    k &= 127;
    if (k == 0) return x;

    if (k < 64) {
        uint64_t hi = (x.hi << k) | (x.lo >> (64 - k));
        uint64_t lo = (x.lo << k) | (x.hi >> (64 - k));
        return (uint128){hi, lo};
    } else {
        unsigned k2 = k - 64;
        uint64_t hi = (x.lo << k2) | (x.hi >> (64 - k2));
        uint64_t lo = (x.hi << k2) | (x.lo >> (64 - k2));
        return (uint128){hi, lo};
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of deeper bit-manipulation: 3D Morton ordering for spatial indexing, with no loops and tight constants.

## Key Concepts
- Morton (z-order) encoding across 3 dimensions
- bit spreading (part1by2) with masks/shifts
- avoiding branches for tight hot-paths

## Code Example
```javascript
function part1by2(n){
  let x = BigInt(n) & 0x1fffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 32n)) & 0x1f00000000ffffn;
  x = (x | (x << 16n)) & 0x1f0000ff0000ffn;
  x = (x | (x << 8n))  & 0x100f00f00f00f00fn;
  x = (x | (x << 4n))  & 0x10c30c30c30c30c3n;
  x = (x | (x << 2n))  & 0x1249249249249249n;
  return x;
}
function morton3D(x,y,z){
  return part1by2(x) | (part1by2(y) << 1n) | (part1by2(z) << 2n);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt to 32-bit inputs? Trade-offs?
- Could you implement a inverse decode that recovers x,y,z from the code?

